"""Writes index/revenue-household-incidence-model.md from incidence.py and years.py."""
import json
from incidence import ROWS, OPTIONS, K, SCEN, run, gf_test, per_hh, INC, GROUPS, QO, QEDU
from years import series, YRS
m = lambda v: ("+" if v >= 0 else "−") + f"${abs(v):,.0f}"
res = {n: run(o, dr) for n, (o, dr) in SCEN.items()}
gfs = {n: gf_test(o) for n, (o, dr) in SCEN.items()}
key = lambda p: next(n for n in SCEN if n.startswith(p + "."))
Q, A0, QC19, QH5, QSAF, QED = (key(p) for p in ("Q", "A", "Q-C19", "Q-H5p", "Q-SAF", "Q-EDU"))
rq, lines = res[Q]; q4 = lambda n: res[n][0]["Q4"]["net"]
yq = series(QO); yc = series([o if o != "C19b" else "C19" for o in QO]); ye = series(QEDU)
LS = json.load(open("ledger.json"))["summary"]
tn = lambda v: ("−" if v < 0 else "") + f"${abs(v):.2f}T"
o = []; A = o.append
A("# Revenue: D3 household net test (incidence model)\n")
A(f"Espen Swanson · Generated by index/tools/incidence-model.py, incidence-years.py and incidence-make-md.py from the ledger (base {tn(LS['base_revenue']['mid'])}, gap {tn(LS['gap_mid'])}, about {tn(LS['gap_after_accepted'])} after the accepted options). Role: index. Regenerate it rather than editing by hand.\n")
A("This is the test D3 sets: every income group below the top comes out ahead on net from the agenda's combined taxes, fees and benefits, and new general-fund revenue comes mostly from the top 1% and corporations. The unit is the bottom four quintiles, with the top quintile split out. Figures are average dollars per household per year over 2030-39 (nominal), from ten-year totals divided across about 140M households (estimate). Every distribution share is an **estimate** anchored to a published pattern; no row is yet a microsimulation.\n")
A(f"**The decided package (scenario Q)** is the base, Package B (C18, C35, C19b, C20), the top-end sprint package C38 (REV-TS1 to TS11), and C39-C41 (REV-TS12, a 28% cap on itemized deductions above $450K; REV-HP1, the CEO pay-ratio corporate surtax; REV-HP3, an excise on top-officer pay). No program trim is taken, so universal meals (EDU-3) and Medicare dental stay. Every quintile below the top comes out ahead: Q1 {m(rq['Q1']['net'])}, Q2 {m(rq['Q2']['net'])}, Q3 {m(rq['Q3']['net'])} and **Q4 {m(rq['Q4']['net'])}** a household a year on the ten-year average. {gfs[Q][3]}% of new general-fund revenue comes from the top 1% and corporations. The fourth quintile is {m(yq[2030]['Q4'])} in 2030, {m(yq[2031]['Q4'])} in 2031 and {m(yq[2034]['Q4'])} by 2034.\n")
A("**October 24 family adoption:** costs now include FAM-1D/AFA and one million new voucher awards. The existing CTC/housing distribution keys remain provisional proxies; this screen does not implement exact AFA tax losses, new funding or within-quintile effects. A positive aggregate result is not a completed distributional score for the new design.\n")
A("## Bottom line\n")
A(f"- **The retained allocation-key screen is positive on the ten-year average.** The fourth quintile gains about {m(rq['Q4']['net'])} a household a year ({rq['Q4']['pct']}% of income); the first three gain {m(rq['Q1']['net'])}, {m(rq['Q2']['net'])} and {m(rq['Q3']['net'])}.")
A(f"- **The employer health-exclusion cap is the swing.** At the 75th percentile (C19b, decided) the fourth quintile gains {m(rq['Q4']['net'])}; at the median premium (C19, the fallback if the sprint package falls short) {m(q4(QC19))}. The hospital-price pass-through to wages is the assumption most able to flip the result: without it the fourth quintile lands at {m(q4(QH5))}.")
A(f"- **The revenue-mix test passes** in every scenario: the top 1% (by incidence) and corporations (statutory) bear {gfs[Q][3]}% of new general-fund revenue on the decided package and {gfs[A0][3]}% on the base alone; the rule is more than half.")
A(f"- **The 80th-99th percentiles pay about {m(-rq['Q5x']['net']).replace('+','')} a year** on net, where C18 and C20 land, and the top 1% about {m(-rq['T1']['net']).replace('+','')}.")
A(f"- **The education fund (EDU-9B, held) would pass as a closer in place of C19b and C20, but not for free in the fourth quintile.** Its contribution (about 1.4 points of payroll, all counted on workers) and added benefits give Q1-Q4 {m(res[QED][0]['Q1']['net'])}, {m(res[QED][0]['Q2']['net'])}, {m(res[QED][0]['Q3']['net'])} and {m(q4(QED))}. Whether to use a middle-reaching contribution at all is a D3 and D4 decision, deferred to the revenue strategy session on payroll raisers.")
A("- **Averages hide losers inside the fourth quintile.** A household there without children, without marketplace or Medicare coverage, and with employer coverage above the 75th-percentile premium gets few of the benefits and most of the costs. The next version needs within-quintile spread.\n")
A("## Results by scenario\n")
A("Net = benefits minus costs, dollars per household per year, 2030-39 average. Each scenario after Q varies one choice against it; scenario A (the base alone) leaves the gap unfunded and is shown for comparison.\n")
A("| Scenario | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | 80-99th | Top 1% | Top 1% + corporations, share of new GF revenue | Passes? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for n in SCEN:
    r = res[n][0]; ok = all(r[g]["net"] > 0 for g in GROUPS[:4])
    note = ("Yes" if ok else "No (Q4)") + (" (but the gap is unfunded)" if n.startswith("A.") else "")
    A(f"| {n} | {m(r['Q1']['net'])} | {m(r['Q2']['net'])} | {m(r['Q3']['net'])} | {m(r['Q4']['net'])} | {m(r['Q5']['net'])} | {m(r['Q5x']['net'])} | {m(r['T1']['net'])} | {gfs[n][3]}% | {note} |")
A("\n### As a share of income (scenario Q)\n\n| | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Top 1% |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
A("| Benefits | " + " | ".join(f"${rq[g]['benefit']:,}" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("| Costs | " + " | ".join(f"${rq[g]['cost']:,}" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("| Net, % of income | " + " | ".join(f"{rq[g]['pct']:+.1f}%" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("\nHealth benefits are valued at government cost, as CBO does, so the bottom quintile's gain is large relative to cash income. Most of it is coverage, not cash.\n")
A("## What moves the fourth quintile (scenario Q)\n\n| Item | Benefit or cost | Per Q4 household a year |\n| --- | --- | --- |")
items = sorted(((abs(per_hh(d['Q4'], 'Q4')), rid, label, side, per_hh(d['Q4'], 'Q4')) for rid, label, side, amt, d, basis in lines), reverse=True)
for _, rid, label, side, v in items[:25]:
    A(f"| {rid}: {label} | {side} | ${abs(v):,.0f}{' (negative)' if v < 0 else ''} |")
A("")
A("## By year\n")
A("Each ten-year total is spread over 2030-39 by a phase-in profile (index/data/inputs/incidence-phase-in.csv): about 4% annual growth for every row, plus ramps (estimates). Child care reaches the full cap in 2032. The public option opens in 2031 and auto-enrolls in 2032. Medicare hearing and vision start in 2031 and dental last. Paid-leave benefits start in 2031 while contributions start in 2030. Moving America's fuel steps phase in through 2038. The carbon fee, the dividend and the low-income transition share follow the fee's price path. Universal meals start with the 2030-31 school year; national service ramps to its 2034 scale; the public-safety receipts start in 2031. Net dollars per household, nominal.\n")
A("| Year | Q: Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q-C19: Q4 | Q-EDU: Q4 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for y in YRS:
    A(f"| {y} | {m(yq[y]['Q1'])} | {m(yq[y]['Q2'])} | {m(yq[y]['Q3'])} | **{m(yq[y]['Q4'])}** | {m(yc[y]['Q4'])} | {m(ye[y]['Q4'])} |")
first = next((y for y in YRS if all(yq[z]['Q4'] > 0 for z in YRS if z >= y)), None)
y5 = all(yq[2034][g] > 0 for g in GROUPS[:4])
A(f"\n- **The fourth quintile is behind in 2030** on the decided package ({m(yq[2030]['Q4'])}){f', and positive from {first} on' if first else ''}. The early shortfall is the carbon fee, leave contributions and the exclusion cap arriving before the public option and full child care.")
A(f"- **Year five (2034) {'passes' if y5 else 'does not pass'} for every quintile below the top**, D3's interim test. The 2030 shortfall lands just before the November 2030 midterm.")
A("- **Fixing 2030 is a sequencing choice, not a size choice.** The levers: start C19b and the carbon fee in 2031, front-load the Climate Dividend (pay the 2030 dividend at the 2031 rate from a Treasury advance repaid from later receipts), or start leave contributions with benefits in 2031. Each costs about a year of revenue and needs a D4 check.\n")
A("## Open choices this model informs\n\nThese are options with their effects, not edits to any canonical file.\n")
A(f"1. **The 2030 dip.** The sequencing levers above; none is decided.")
A(f"2. **EDU-9B, the education fund (held).** As a closer in place of C19b and C20 it moves the fourth quintile from {m(rq['Q4']['net'])} to {m(q4(QED))}. It needs Espen's call under D3 (a middle-reaching contribution) and D4 (a new dedicated fund).")
A(f"3. **SAF-R2.** Dropping the alcohol excise moves the fourth quintile from {m(rq['Q4']['net'])} to {m(q4(QSAF))} and puts about $0.07T on the top-end base.")
A("4. **The Climate Dividend.** Shifting part of the 20% transition share into the dividend (CLI-2C) helps every quintile equally.")
A("5. **The hospital-price pass-through (H5p)** needs a sourced estimate before the result is final.\n")
A("## Dynamic effects and the D3 test\n")
A(f"The ledger carries a dynamic sensitivity table (not in any total): under CBO/JCT-style conventions macroeconomic feedback is about {tn(LS['dynamic_mid'])} over 2030-39 ({tn(LS['dynamic_lo'])} to {tn(LS['dynamic_hi'])}), and about {tn(LS['dynamic_optimistic'])} under Talarico-style assumptions (estimates). It does not change the quintile results above, for three reasons. **Most of it is revenue, not household money:** the largest row is lower investment and more avoidance by corporations and the top 1%, which lands on the top quintile and the gap, not on the bottom four. **The household-facing pieces roughly offset:** the care labor-supply gain raises earnings mostly in the second to fourth quintiles, while the small labor-supply drag from coverage and the Child Tax Credit falls on the same groups through slightly lower hours, a choice the household makes rather than a cost imposed on it. **Wage effects of corporate taxes are already in:** the model puts 25% of corporate taxes on workers (the JCT/CBO convention), which is the channel a dynamic score would otherwise add. If a dynamic gap had to be closed with middle-reaching revenue (C19 or C20) rather than top-end options, that choice would move the quintiles and should be run as its own scenario.\n")
A("## Limits\n\n- **Shares are estimates.** Each row uses a pattern key; replacing them with published TPC and CBO tables is the next version.\n- **Year profiles are shapes, not scored annual paths.**\n- **No behavior.** Coverage shifts, avoidance beyond the ledger's 15% haircuts, and macro effects are left out (the education session's maternal-employment effect, plausibly $25-45B a year of federal revenue at maturity, is not counted).\n- **Public goods are left out** on both sides: the agency rebuild, transit service, climate credits to firms, defense, education research (EDU-8) and the civic and information rows.\n- **Full pass-through** of carbon, border, transport and alcohol charges to households, which is conservative for the middle. EDU-9B's contribution is counted entirely on workers.\n- **Corporate taxes** use the JCT/CBO convention: 25% labor, 75% capital.\n- **Not modeled:** the minimum wage (LAB-4), the H-1B fee, COM-5's bank-fee limits (a household benefit), veterans health (NSC-10), and immigration's legalization effects.\n")
A("## Model inputs\n\n| ID | Instrument | Side | 2030-39 ($T) | Key | Basis |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for rid, label, side, amt, parts, basis in ROWS:
    A(f"| {rid} | {label} | {side} | {amt} | {', '.join(k for k, _ in parts)} | {basis} |")
for oid, (side, amt, parts, label) in OPTIONS.items():
    A(f"| {oid} | {label} | {side} | {amt} | {', '.join(k for k, _ in parts)} | ledger option |")
A("\n**Distribution keys** (percent of the total by quintile, Q1 to Q5, and the top 1%'s share of the total). All are estimates.\n\n| Key | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Top 1% |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for k, (sh, t1) in K.items(): A(f"| {k} | " + " | ".join(str(x) for x in sh) + f" | {t1} |")
A("\nOther assumptions (estimates): about 140M households mid-window; 2034 average incomes of $23K, $60K, $105K, $180K and $520K by quintile, and $4.3M for the top 1%; 30% of Medicare Advantage capture passes to enrollees as fewer extras; 30% of clean-energy credits go to households; $0.30T of Part B premiums; $0.50T of hospital-price savings passed to wages. EDU-9B: ~1.4 points of uncapped payroll at ~$135B a point in 2030 growing 4% ($2.27T in 2030-39); $0.63T of benefits beyond the chosen rows (pre-K and child care share $0.24T; teacher floor, PARA and mental-health staff, Title I and IDEA top-ups $0.39T), from domains/domain-education.md.\n")
A("## Sources\n\n- [CBO, The Distribution of Household Income, 2022](https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62300)\n- [CBO budget option, Reduce Tax Subsidies for Employment-Based Health Benefits](https://www.cbo.gov/budget-options/60953)\n- [Fremstad and Paul, PERI WP434](https://peri.umass.edu/images/publication/WP434.pdf)\n- [TPC TaxVox, Who benefits from health-care related tax expenditures?](https://taxpolicycenter.org/taxvox/who-benefits-health-care-related-tax-expenditures)\n- [Tax Foundation on JCT's corporate incidence convention](https://taxfoundation.org/jct-corporate-tax-falls-partly-labor/)\n- [CBO budget option 52284, alcohol at $16 per proof gallon (2016)](https://www.cbo.gov/budget-options/2016/52284)\n- sources/education-transforming-education-act-analysis-and-trust-fund.md: EDU-3's increment and EDU-9B's rates and spending paths\n- index/2029-trifecta-master-ledger.md and the domain files for every amount\n")
open("revenue-household-incidence-model.md", "w").write("\n".join(o)); print(len("\n".join(o)))
